Z||000013||Old Lar||03-05-2001||07:21 AM||||Haze:  Bob Stieger at Twin Lakes is in Paxton, Ill.  Bob has worked on both my Fieros, his prices are reasonable and he's a lot closer to you than he is to me.  Maybe 100 miles down the road.  He has a flatbed truck if the car is not drivable. Bob is in the vendor list if you want to contact him.<P>I threw my arms up a long time ago at trying to do mechanics on my Fieros. It is costly for me to go to Mr. Goodwrench,  but I don't have to tools, space, time, skill or patience to work on my cars any more.  Climbing under a car in my drivway is no longer the joy it used to be.||||reg||3
Z||000014||87GTZ34||03-05-2001||02:49 PM||||Haze - Off the cuff, it does sound like the distributor or something inside of it.  1) A grinding noise (how loud is it); 2) a jumpy timing mark; 3) doesn't want to idle. <P>How many miles on the engine? The only other thing I could think of timing gear/chain that would give a grinding noise and jumpy timing.||||reg||1
Z||000015||fierospeeder||03-05-2001||05:09 PM||||Haze, i live about 30min away, so if orief finds the problem, i can get parts for you at the local junkyard.  I know two junkyards with numerous four cylinder fieros. and i can get parts for a v6 also.<P>Thats the way i diagnois my car, getting parts from the yard and see if it makes a difference.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000016||Cplensdorf||03-05-2001||08:55 PM||||My previous V6 spun the Harmonic balancer.  This threw off the timing mark (and I readjusted it in error and ran like crap).  It took a long time to figure this out.  Tried everything.  Same scraping noise, though.  It was outside ring of the balancer wearing a hole in the timing chain cover.  Hope this helps!<P>||||reg||1
